{"name":"Proper Temperature","hex":"#ede5c7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"proper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Suitable.\nSuited or acceptable to the purpose or circumstances; fit, suitable. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Suited or acceptable to the purpose or circumstances; fit, suitable. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Properly; thoroughly; completely.","Properly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/proper"},{"term":"temperature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A measure of cold or heat, often measurable with a thermometer.","An elevated body temperature, as present in many illnesses; fever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/temperature"}],"entries":[{"title":"Temperature","blocks":[{"text":"In classical thermodynamics and kinetic theory, temperature reflects the average kinetic energy of the particles in a system, providing a quantitative measure of how energy is distributed among microscopic degrees of freedom."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/23/Thermally_Agitated_Molecule.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temperature"},{"title":"Color temperature","blocks":[{"text":"Color temperature is a parameter describing the color of a visible light source by comparing it to the color of light emitted by an idealized opaque, non-reflective body, known as a black body. The temperature of the ideal emitter that matches the color most closely is defined as the color temperature of the original visible light source. The color temperature scale describes only the color of light emitted by a light source, which may actually be at a different temperature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/PlanckianLocus.png/330px-PlanckianLocus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_temperature"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Pakkanen.jpg/500px-Pakkanen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Pakkanen.jpg/500px-Pakkanen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A typical Celsius thermometer measures a winter day temperature of −17 °C.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APakkanen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Translational_motion.gif/250px-Translational_motion.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Translational_motion.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Translational_motion.gif/250px-Translational_motion.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A theoretical understanding of temperature in a hard-sphere model of a gas can be obtained from the Kinetic theory.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATranslational_motion.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Gas_thermometer_and_absolute_zero.jpg/500px-Gas_thermometer_and_absolute_zero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Gas_thermometer_and_absolute_zero.jpg/500px-Gas_thermometer_and_absolute_zero.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plots of pressure vs temperature for three different gas samples extrapolated to absolute zero","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGas_thermometer_and_absolute_zero.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedi